  "markdown": "## Overview\nCostco Wholesale Corp. operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites, offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products [10-K line 00134]. The company's business model is centered around its membership model, which drives customer loyalty and generates steady fee revenue with high renewal rates (92.3% in U.S. and Canada) [10-K line 00167]. It competes globally with a wide range of retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, and discounters [10-K line 00190], differentiating itself through low prices, high-quality merchandise, and limited SKUs (less than 4,000 active SKUs per warehouse in core business) [10-K line 00141]. Key financial highlights for fiscal year 2026 include net sales of $297.2 billion, a 10% increase, membership fee revenue of $5.9 billion (up 11%), and net income of $9.226 billion [MD&A line 00416, MD&A line 00418, MD&A line 00422].\n\n## Market Positioning and Growth Outlook\nCostco positions itself as a low-cost provider with a strong emphasis on customer loyalty through its membership model. It differentiates from competitors like Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon by offering limited SKUs, private-label brands such as Kirkland Signature, and high-quality merchandise [10-K line 00192]. The company's growth drivers include comparable sales growth, expansion of new warehouses (net increase of 25 in fiscal year 2026), membership fee revenue, particularly from Executive memberships, and the continued growth of e-commerce, which represented 11% of total net sales in 2026 [MD&A line 00415, MD&A line 00459, 10-K line 00153]. Future prospects are supported by international expansion, membership fee increases, and operational efficiencies derived from its volume purchasing power and cross-docking distribution methods [10-K line 00134, 10-K line 00135].",
